  ],
  "question": {
    "text": "Let a common tangent to the curves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math> touch the curves at the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math> is equal to ________.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "32",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given,</p><p>Common tangent to the curves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math> touch the curves at the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math></p><p>Let, the equation of tangent be,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;is focus and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is slope,</p><p>So, equation of tangent to parabola will be,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Also line is tangent to circle,</p><p>So, using perpendicular distance formula from centre&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>to tangent which will be radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>, we get,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></math></p><p>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#177;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>Now if&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;then&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Now equation of tangent will be,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>And&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;will be foot of perpendicular&nbsp;from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> on tangent<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> ,</p><p>So, using foot of perpendicular formula&nbsp; we get,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Hence, by distance formula we get,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></math></p></blockquote>",
